"comparison": "Both JPMorganChase and Bank of America disclose that their trading and derivatives businesses face significant losses from unanticipated market events, specifically highlighting the risk that previously uncorrelated factors may become correlated. JPMorganChase notes that its market-making and investment portfolio businesses can suffer losses from such events, which may reduce profitability and liquidity (jpm_2024_1A_P052_S001, jpm_2024_1A_P053_S001). Similarly, Bank of America states that fluctuations in asset values or unanticipated credit events, including unforeseen correlations, may lead to unexpected losses in its derivatives businesses (bac_2024_1A_P051_S001, bac_2024_1A_P051_S002). While JPMorgan emphasizes the impact on investment portfolio and hedging effectiveness, Bank of America focuses on the contractual risks and collateral requirements associated with these derivatives exposures.",
